/ref 1079 #uuid "e13459e6-807d-540e-845c-f7ab2efc8cf6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478441 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97861" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478441 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478441 #edb/ref 1071 "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ invertible matrix, then which of the following could be the characteristic equation of $C?$\n\nI. ${\\lambda }^{4}+2{\\lambda }^{3}+3{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda =0$\n\nII. $\\lambda (\\lambda -2)^{2}(\\lambda +3)=0$\n\nIII. ${\\lambda }^{4}-3{\\lambda }^{2}+4=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc0c0d74-29c3-5a54-b40b-f1888e3dbce2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478441 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $C$ is invertible, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $0$ can't be an eigenvalue of $C.$ As a result, $\\lambda =0$ should not be a solution to the corresponding characteristic equation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given equations in turn.\n\n- Equation I **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $C$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation II **cannot** be the characteristic equation of $C$ since $\\lambda =0$ is a root of the equation.\n\n- Equation III could  be the characteristic equation of $C.$ Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-97971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478454 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478454 #edb/ref 1071 "If $B$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(B+3I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}$\n\nII. $B+3I$ is singular\n\nIII. $\\lambda =-3$ is an eigenvalue of $B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4997f39-9351-5840-b1b4-1804fe6c9f0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478454 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(B+3I) & =2-\\text{rank}(B+3I) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{Null}(B+3I)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statements II is false. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(B+3I)=0,$ we obtain that $B+3I$ must be non-singular.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $M=B+3I$ is non-singular, then $(B+3I)x=0$ has only zero solution. As a result, $\\lambda =-3$ can not be an eigenvalue of $B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487555 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186488290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487555 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186488301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487555 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186488313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487555 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186488316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487555 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186488346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487555 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487555 #edb/ref 1045 "The Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487555 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186488313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487555 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487555 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050470 #edb/ref 175