079 #uuid "93a75aae-536a-560a-b748-38762d5ef8b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488246 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488246 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488246 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488246 #edb/ref 1071 "If $C$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $(C-5I)x=0$ has only zero solution\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488246 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a57f1e60-3a1f-5809-920d-a2e3527a6835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488246 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a $4\\times 4$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=4=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=C-5I.$\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that if $\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(C-5I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $C.$ As a result, the matrix $M=C-5I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are true. Since $M=C-5I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(C-5I)\\ne {R}^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(C-5I)\\ne \\{0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488290 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186488301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488290 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0a855166-560a-5030-8b45-18d477914230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488290 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186362257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488301 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "44b1d683-1553-59db-aadd-0a5e6069911a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488301 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186488246 #edb/ref 1